From 5a307a4652be73a41f5124fee0b536fbaa3808a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:59:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: create a diagnostic report, and turn the diagnostics
 on and off
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

What a problem report needed was spread over three places: Submit Log
(Kodi's log and the configuration), the diagnostics of each start on the
boot partition (only while their switch was on, and the switch was a file
on that partition that only the installer for Windows renamed), and
"Copy the diagnostics to a USB drive". A person who is asked for a
diagnostic had to know all three.

System > Submit Log has two entries more:

- "Create a diagnostic report", first: /usr/lib/hades-diag/report.sh now
  collects what the diagnostics collect, at once (the graphics and the
  displays, the logs of Kodi and of the system, the journal of this start
  and of the start before it, the newest crash log of Kodi), puts every
  file through the filter of the diagnostics, adds what they saved at the
  last starts, and keeps it as one zip file in /storage/logfiles. The bar
  in a corner of the screen says that it is being made; then the name and
  the size of the file, and whether to copy it to a USB drive now. The
  copy goes to the top of the drive, read back from it before it is said
  to be there (to-usb.sh file); with no drive plugged in, it can be
  plugged in and tried again. Without the copy, a text says where the file
  is and how to take it (the file manager, the share Logfiles, SSH), and
  in live mode that it is gone when the system is turned off. Each reason
  of the script for not making it, or for not copying it, is said for what
  it is. The script is run outside the environment of Kodi, in a process
  group of its own, and stopped after 10 minutes (it bounds each of its
  own commands).
- "Save diagnostics at every start": the switch of the diagnostics, read
  from the boot partition each time the list is shown and kept nowhere
  else (/usr/lib/hades-diag/switch.sh status, on, off: DIAG-OFF.TXT and
  DIAG-ON.TXT, as the installer renames them). Its help says in plain words
  what it saves, where and how much, that it is filtered and sent nowhere,
  and that it begins with the next start: the help of a setting is a box
  three lines high that does not scroll, so once it is turned on a text
  to be read at the reader's pace says the whole of it (what it saves at
  each start; the last eight starts, usually 1 to 5 MB each; nothing with
  less than 128 MB free; the journal of each start in the storage, 16 MB
  at most; the filter; how to take what it saved). Turned on with less
  than 128 MB free on the partition, that is said. A change that the
  partition did not take is said, and the list shows what the partition
  says.

"There are no diagnostics on this system to copy" (33102) names the
setting as the way to turn them on.

Texts in English and the three Spanish ones (tú in Spain and Mexico, vos
in Argentina; "vídeo" in Spain; "log" in Mexico; "sitio", "lugar" and
"espacio" for room), numbered from 33600 and placed after 33116, apart
from the numbers of the other changes of this version. The switch comes
after "Check the Dolby Vision output" in the list.
